{"title":"2025 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 10","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A small square is drawn inside a larger square, as shown.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The area of the shaded region and the area of the unshaded region are each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/2/682dde7165fdc1500b05f16ab01da0086efa525d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$18\\mbox{ cm}^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"17\" />. What is the side length of the larger square?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/1/ce1556ae319759f35f6f02a27785092509cdad48.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 3 \\text{ cm} \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 4 \\text { cm} \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 6 \\text { cm} \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 9 \\text{ cm} \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 12 \\text { cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"531\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can notice that the area of the shaded region and the area of the unshaded region add up to the area of the large square. These areas are also the same, so the area of the large <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Square\" title=\"Square\">square</a> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/b/e5b045686ac4f9276ab5406fbcc421559b6bd7b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$18 \\text{ cm}^{2} \\times 2 = 36 \\text { cm}^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"162\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> be the side length of the large square. The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Area\" title=\"Area\">area</a> of any square is equal to the square of its side length, so we have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/2/f2234dc941489913734489fd3c53294fad393752.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s = 36 \\text{ cm}^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/6/cf69821b9644729329dcadaf765f119c34a54a16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s = \\sqrt{36 \\text { cm}^{2}} = \\boxed{\\textbf {(C) } 6 \\text { cm}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"209\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Pascal&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2025 CEMC Pascal (page does not exist)\">2025 CEMC Pascal</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Pascal_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2025 CEMC Pascal Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Pascal_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2025 CEMC Pascal Answer Key (page does not exis